WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.652 --> 00:00:03.485
(pulsating music)

00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:20.353
- [Narrator] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:23.065
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:23.065 --> 00:00:24.842
- We could just be taken out at any time,

00:00:24.842 --> 00:00:25.745
no doubt about it.

00:00:25.745 --> 00:00:28.193
- [Narrator] For others,
when weather strikes

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:30.193
inspiration begins.

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:32.052
- Dude this is it, this is it, right here.

00:00:32.052 --> 00:00:33.635
If they get a wave.

00:00:34.495 --> 00:00:37.705
- [Narrator] These are the
people who challenge nature,

00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:41.535
seek out its limits, reveal its secrets,

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:44.035
and embrace its awesome power.

00:00:47.516 --> 00:00:51.584
In this episode, we'll meet
an explorer scaling peaks

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.935
in the most remote edges of the world

00:00:53.935 --> 00:00:56.375
to go where no one has gone before.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:00:57.935
- [Man] If I make a mistake on this climb

00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:00.324
or on this expedition, I die.

00:01:00.324 --> 00:01:01.724
- [Narrator] An artist
who created the world's

00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:03.448
first underwater museum

00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:06.128
to give struggling marine life a new home.

00:01:06.128 --> 00:01:08.720
- [Man] You can't get a better
co-collaborator than nature.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.528
- [Narrator] And a surf
photographer who battled

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:13.579
violent conditions and
near constant darkness

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:16.280
in search of the perfect image.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.686
These pioneers of the great outdoors ahead

00:01:18.686 --> 00:01:20.353
on "That's Amazing."

00:01:29.388 --> 00:01:31.839
Adventure photographer Chris Burkhard is

00:01:31.839 --> 00:01:34.213
an expert in photographing surfers

00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:37.994
riding the coldest, most
punishing waves on the planet.

00:01:37.994 --> 00:01:40.774
He's accustomed to battling
the elements in his work.

00:01:40.774 --> 00:01:44.085
But one storm in Iceland nearly broke him.

00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:45.754
- It was like Armageddon.

00:01:45.754 --> 00:01:47.693
We had basically just been, you know,

00:01:47.693 --> 00:01:49.535
totally beaten to shreds.

00:01:49.535 --> 00:01:51.535
I mean, it was total darkness.

00:01:51.535 --> 00:01:54.165
- [Narrator] The storm
canceled Burkhard's expedition,

00:01:54.165 --> 00:01:56.045
but the decision to stay in Iceland

00:01:56.045 --> 00:01:59.375
resulted in a once-in-a-lifetime
photographic opportunity

00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:01.386
that he never expected.

00:02:01.386 --> 00:02:04.165
- Dude, this is it, this is it, right here.

00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:07.004
- [Narrator] He managed to
capture an epic adventure

00:02:07.004 --> 00:02:10.223
under the greatest light show on Earth.

00:02:10.223 --> 00:02:12.383
- If you look at the
Northern Lights and you don't

00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:14.714
question your place in the world,

00:02:14.714 --> 00:02:16.853
then there's something dead inside you.

00:02:16.853 --> 00:02:19.436
I can't believe that is heaven!

00:02:24.215 --> 00:02:26.274
I grew up kind of frolicking
along the coast here

00:02:26.274 --> 00:02:27.695
in Central California.

00:02:27.695 --> 00:02:30.345
I would drive up the coast and
try to surf with my friends

00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:32.212
in these remote little beaches and stuff.

00:02:32.212 --> 00:02:34.303
And to be honest, like, that really

00:02:34.303 --> 00:02:36.564
set the precedent for me that like, I

00:02:36.564 --> 00:02:39.544
wanted to just go explore.

00:02:39.544 --> 00:02:41.047
I first picked up a camera

00:02:41.047 --> 00:02:44.775
right around the age I think of 18, 19.

00:02:44.775 --> 00:02:46.516
I realized that this was it, this was like

00:02:46.516 --> 00:02:48.764
the perfect medium for expression.

00:02:48.764 --> 00:02:51.647
Like this was as good as it could get.

00:02:51.647 --> 00:02:53.195
When I grew up I didn't have a passport,

00:02:53.195 --> 00:02:54.695
never traveled internationally.

00:02:54.695 --> 00:02:57.055
And as I realized that
photography could be this tool

00:02:57.055 --> 00:02:59.015
to see the world, I was like, oh my gosh,

00:02:59.015 --> 00:03:02.607
like this is it, this could be my ticket.

00:03:02.607 --> 00:03:04.375
- [Narrator] After years of photographing

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:07.114
in stunning beach towns around the world,

00:03:07.114 --> 00:03:11.234
Burkhard decided to shift
his focus to colder climates.

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:14.443
- I had the dream job,
and then I realized that

00:03:14.443 --> 00:03:17.874
I want to document places that
might not be around forever

00:03:17.874 --> 00:03:20.233
and I wanna share the experience of

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.085
the importance of seeing these places.

00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:23.485
Because I want people to know that

00:03:23.485 --> 00:03:25.735
these places are out there.

00:03:28.304 --> 00:03:30.216
There's been a lot of zero-degree days.

00:03:30.216 --> 00:03:32.384
A lot of days where you're
standing on the beach

00:03:32.384 --> 00:03:34.064
or shooting somewhere on a mountain side

00:03:34.064 --> 00:03:35.894
and you're just, your organs are hurting

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:36.925
cuz it's so cold.

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:38.936
And you're touching metal equipment

00:03:38.936 --> 00:03:42.376
and your hands are just
getting brittle and cracked.

00:03:42.376 --> 00:03:44.135
It's type two fun, you know, you look back

00:03:44.135 --> 00:03:47.304
and you chuckle but in the
moment it's pretty brutal,

00:03:47.304 --> 00:03:50.016
you want to be somewhere else.

00:03:50.016 --> 00:03:51.805
My work has taken me
to some of the harshest

00:03:51.805 --> 00:03:53.085
environments on the planet.

00:03:53.085 --> 00:03:55.323
And I seek out some of
the last places on Earth

00:03:55.323 --> 00:03:59.406
where you can really find
these raw environments.

00:04:04.094 --> 00:04:06.165
- [Narrator] Of all the places he's been,

00:04:06.165 --> 00:04:08.085
one trip to Iceland pushed him closest

00:04:08.085 --> 00:04:10.064
to his breaking point.

00:04:10.064 --> 00:04:13.434
What started off as a trip
to photograph epic waves

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:16.767
became an epic battle with the elements.

00:04:19.374 --> 00:04:21.064
- This last winter during the darkest and

00:04:21.064 --> 00:04:23.784
coldest time of year,
we set out to Iceland.

00:04:23.784 --> 00:04:27.445
And I didn't really have
any idea of how it would go.

00:04:27.445 --> 00:04:29.565
There was this one location in this

00:04:29.565 --> 00:04:33.314
remote national park that
I wanted to go access.

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:35.362
All I knew is that
there was a boat captain

00:04:35.362 --> 00:04:38.195
that was willing to take us there.

00:04:39.932 --> 00:04:44.034
We finally made it to that
remote bay, this national park.

00:04:44.034 --> 00:04:45.000
We're sitting there in the boat

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.295
and we're looking at this, this wave.

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:49.055
It was everything we had hoped for.

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.661
The captain came out and said that the

00:04:50.661 --> 00:04:54.303
largest storm in 25 years
was coming right now,

00:04:54.303 --> 00:04:57.005
and we literally had to turn
and high tail out of there

00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:58.588
back to the harbor.

00:05:04.181 --> 00:05:06.022
The weather changed like
that (snaps finger).

00:05:06.022 --> 00:05:07.257
and it was like Armageddon.

00:05:07.257 --> 00:05:08.889
People were just like getting food storage

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:10.040
and stocking up.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.250
And all the flights were
grounded so we made this decision

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.488
that we were gonna drive
11 hours to the next

00:05:14.488 --> 00:05:17.261
safe town so we don't all
come home empty handed.

00:05:17.261 --> 00:05:20.060
We were all just defeated but still knew

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.549
there was like something
else out there for us.

00:05:21.549 --> 00:05:24.949
And we just knew that we
wanted to play it out.

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:28.589
That was probably not the smartest call.

00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:30.490
And we weren't where
we originally planned.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.781
We were like forced to
stay a little bit longer.

00:05:32.781 --> 00:05:35.520
And after that storm
subsided we realized that

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.130
with that storm brought incredible surf.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.549
And not only that but the
Northern Lights were out.

00:05:40.549 --> 00:05:42.765
If the weather could just hold off for

00:05:42.765 --> 00:05:45.477
that much longer, this
would be something that

00:05:45.477 --> 00:05:47.977
would be in the history books.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.081
I convinced one of the guys to go out.

00:05:57.081 --> 00:05:59.132
The hardest thing was
just keeping my focus.

00:05:59.132 --> 00:06:01.241
What I was seeing, you know, was these

00:06:01.241 --> 00:06:05.450
amazing patterns of blue
and orange and red and green

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.481
you know, trickling in the sky.

00:06:07.481 --> 00:06:09.222
This is maybe one of
the most insane things

00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:11.582
I've ever seen in my life.

00:06:11.582 --> 00:06:14.732
So every so often I'd get
lost just staring at the sky.

00:06:14.732 --> 00:06:16.721
And somebody would holler
and I would look and see,

00:06:16.721 --> 00:06:18.999
and I'd be like okay, okay, here it is.

00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:21.132
There we go, Ben, someone's going.

00:06:21.132 --> 00:06:22.121
You're shivering, you're sitting there

00:06:22.121 --> 00:06:23.862
and you're kind of like,
basically going through

00:06:23.862 --> 00:06:25.233
all of the emotions.

00:06:25.233 --> 00:06:27.062
But for me, anything
worth pursuing is gonna

00:06:27.062 --> 00:06:28.961
require you to suffer a little bit.

00:06:28.961 --> 00:06:31.781
(men laugh)
Oh, we did it!

00:06:31.781 --> 00:06:36.341
I can't believe that I actually
got you to go out there.

00:06:36.341 --> 00:06:38.350
Through all that we came
away with this experience

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.534
that was actually far better than anything

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:43.258
we could have ever planned for.

00:06:43.258 --> 00:06:47.879
It changed everything I thought
about what was possible.

00:06:47.879 --> 00:06:51.538
I aim nothing more than to simply just

00:06:51.538 --> 00:06:54.247
document the beauty of the world.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:57.027
If a photograph that somebody
sees is powerful enough

00:06:57.027 --> 00:06:59.239
to make them go somewhere
they've never been,

00:06:59.239 --> 00:07:02.119
be outside of their comfort
zone, spend their time.

00:07:02.119 --> 00:07:07.026
That's what I speak to is
like, how we can do that more.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:09.078
I guess we wanna just
inspire future generations

00:07:09.078 --> 00:07:10.621
to be willing to thrust themselves into

00:07:10.621 --> 00:07:12.709
situations like that, be willing to go

00:07:12.709 --> 00:07:14.781
when everything else says not to.

00:07:14.781 --> 00:07:17.069
Even when all the odds
are stacked against you,

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:20.486
you can still create something beautiful.

00:07:32.748 --> 00:07:34.450
- [Ramona] Every day,
whenever I hear that buzzer,

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.999
it just gets your blood
flowing, your heart pumping.

00:07:36.999 --> 00:07:39.461
And you just take off with a sprint.

00:07:39.461 --> 00:07:41.740
You really don't know
what you're getting into

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.749
until you can see the fire.

00:07:44.749 --> 00:07:46.059
You're jumping out of a plane,

00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:50.226
that's always gonna induce
a little bit of adrenaline.

00:07:55.028 --> 00:07:57.488
- [Narrator] There are few
professions that captivate us

00:07:57.488 --> 00:07:59.079
like smoke jumping.

00:07:59.079 --> 00:08:01.049
And this is a woman who's been doing it

00:08:01.049 --> 00:08:02.751
for over a decade.

00:08:02.751 --> 00:08:06.206
My name is Ramona Beyuka and
I am a McCall smokejumper

00:08:06.206 --> 00:08:07.748
in McCall, Idaho.

00:08:07.748 --> 00:08:09.918
- [Narrator] The job of a
firefighter is dangerous.

00:08:09.918 --> 00:08:12.958
But jumping out of a
plane to contain a blaze

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:15.106
takes this job to a whole new level.

00:08:15.106 --> 00:08:16.588
- [Ramona] There's always a possibility

00:08:16.588 --> 00:08:18.034
that something could go wrong so

00:08:18.034 --> 00:08:20.771
they definitely still get nervous.

00:08:20.771 --> 00:08:23.095
We fly in a Twin Otter with the door off.

00:08:23.095 --> 00:08:24.935
And we're all wearing ear plugs, and

00:08:24.935 --> 00:08:27.286
everyone's kind of in their
own world at that point.

00:08:27.286 --> 00:08:29.485
- [Narrator] And this flight
can take up to a few hours,

00:08:29.485 --> 00:08:32.415
but it becomes clear
when the danger is close.

00:08:32.415 --> 00:08:34.346
- [Ramona] Usually you smell that smoke.

00:08:34.346 --> 00:08:36.865
It's that piney,
wood-burning smell, you know

00:08:36.865 --> 00:08:38.365
that it's go time.

00:08:39.647 --> 00:08:43.015
You get in the door and
your heart is pounding and

00:08:43.015 --> 00:08:44.375
I don't even really think about it,

00:08:44.375 --> 00:08:46.625
I just go out of the plane.

00:08:49.516 --> 00:08:54.375
I try not to look at the ground
or the fire at that point.

00:08:54.375 --> 00:08:56.792
If you're just super focused.

00:09:00.207 --> 00:09:02.175
- [Narrator] Now they fight the fire.

00:09:02.175 --> 00:09:06.095
And despite the risks, it's
a job Ramona loves doing.

00:09:06.095 --> 00:09:07.895
- [Ramona] I've been
to so many forest fires

00:09:07.895 --> 00:09:09.836
and every jump is different.

00:09:09.836 --> 00:09:13.076
It is extremely dynamic out there.

00:09:13.076 --> 00:09:14.556
That adrenaline is something that,

00:09:14.556 --> 00:09:17.255
that's a big part of
keeping me coming back.

00:09:17.255 --> 00:09:19.735
I do stop and think, wow, I am really lucky

00:09:19.735 --> 00:09:21.235
to be in this job.

00:09:33.946 --> 00:09:36.213
- [Narrator] High in the
mountains of Ecuador,

00:09:36.213 --> 00:09:40.001
one man is continuing the
500-year-old tradition

00:09:40.001 --> 00:09:42.168
of harvesting glacial ice.

00:09:45.703 --> 00:09:48.413
Reaching the top of
Mount Chimborazo by foot

00:09:48.413 --> 00:09:51.559
is a treacherous all-day journey.

00:09:51.559 --> 00:09:55.009
It is not for the faint of heart.

00:09:55.009 --> 00:09:58.551
And although the demand for
glacial ice is dwindling,

00:09:58.551 --> 00:10:01.751
73-year-old Baltazar Ushca Tenesaca

00:10:01.751 --> 00:10:03.834
refuses to call it quits.

00:10:05.375 --> 00:10:08.875
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:10:22.997 --> 00:10:26.222
- [Rodrigo] Chimborazo is a
dormant volcano in Ecuador.

00:10:26.222 --> 00:10:28.979
It's higher than Mt. Everest if measured

00:10:28.979 --> 00:10:31.520
from the center of the Earth.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.032
The traditional harvesting
the ice in this region

00:10:35.032 --> 00:10:37.805
began with the Spanish
during the colonial times.

00:10:37.805 --> 00:10:40.741
They would send the native
people to retrieve the ice,

00:10:40.741 --> 00:10:43.332
to use it to make ice
cream, to cool the drinks,

00:10:43.332 --> 00:10:45.665
and even to keep meat fresh.

00:10:47.923 --> 00:10:51.423
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:11:01.035 --> 00:11:04.597
Twenty, thirty years ago, there
were still many people

00:11:04.597 --> 00:11:07.247
who were going up every day of the week,

00:11:07.247 --> 00:11:09.148
bringing the ice to the lowlands.

00:11:09.148 --> 00:11:11.677
Now Baltazar is the last one because

00:11:11.677 --> 00:11:14.288
people make their own
ice or they buy the ice

00:11:14.288 --> 00:11:16.205
from the ice factories.

00:11:17.039 --> 00:11:19.787
- [Narrator] But Baltazar
continues to work

00:11:19.787 --> 00:11:21.760
because glacial ice is unique.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:25.260
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:11:50.794 --> 00:11:52.645
- [Rodrigo] I knew when I met Baltazar

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:55.626
that this was something very, very special.

00:11:55.626 --> 00:11:59.346
It was something that you
don't see in any other place.

00:11:59.346 --> 00:12:02.685
To do physical work at
that altitude, uh, is amazing.

00:12:02.685 --> 00:12:06.175
It's amazing, that makes
all of Baltazar's work,

00:12:06.175 --> 00:12:10.342
I think, one of the hardest
works in Earth, really.

00:12:16.138 --> 00:12:19.638
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:13:34.444 --> 00:13:36.928
We call Chimborazo "Taita Chimborazo,"

00:13:36.928 --> 00:13:39.016
which is Father Chimborazo.

00:13:39.016 --> 00:13:41.706
And being the highest volcano in Ecuador,

00:13:41.706 --> 00:13:45.810
you know we have a lot of
respect and love for Chimborazo.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:47.311
- [Narrator] In the nearly 60 years

00:13:47.311 --> 00:13:49.724
Baltazar has been harvesting ice,

00:13:49.724 --> 00:13:52.270
conditions in the region
have changed dramatically

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:53.919
due to climate change.

00:13:53.919 --> 00:13:57.419
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:14:07.373 --> 00:14:09.408
- [Rodrigo] It has become warmer.

00:14:09.408 --> 00:14:11.324
The glaciers are receding at the rate of

00:14:11.324 --> 00:14:13.502
20 to 30 meters a year.

00:14:13.502 --> 00:14:17.671
The conditions around the
ice mine are very harsh.

00:14:17.671 --> 00:14:19.972
He works right under the big rocks.

00:14:19.972 --> 00:14:22.867
With the melting of the
ice those rocks get loose

00:14:22.867 --> 00:14:24.272
and they roll down.

00:14:24.272 --> 00:14:27.361
So yeah, there are danger of avalanches.

00:14:27.361 --> 00:14:31.351
As he grows older, I
worry for Baltazar, yeah.

00:14:31.351 --> 00:14:33.262
- [Narrator] But Baltazar does not worry.

00:14:33.262 --> 00:14:35.597
His only concern is delivering ice to his

00:14:35.597 --> 00:14:39.347
remaining few vendors
in the Riobamba market.

00:14:41.942 --> 00:14:45.442
(Baltazar speaks Spanish)

00:16:11.901 --> 00:16:14.295
During the annual crab migration,

00:16:14.295 --> 00:16:17.462
all of Christmas Island is seeing red.

00:16:20.091 --> 00:16:23.017
Christmas Island, located
in the Pacific Ocean

00:16:23.017 --> 00:16:27.473
near Australia, is known for
its warm, tropical climate.

00:16:27.473 --> 00:16:30.607
Throughout most of the
year it's endemic red crabs

00:16:30.607 --> 00:16:33.353
can be found on the rainforest floor.

00:16:33.353 --> 00:16:36.003
But each year in late
October, the adult crabs

00:16:36.003 --> 00:16:38.645
begin a spectacular mass migration

00:16:38.645 --> 00:16:41.437
from the rainforest to the coast to breed

00:16:41.437 --> 00:16:44.353
and release eggs into the sea.

00:16:44.353 --> 00:16:48.379
This synchronized breeding
of about 40-million crabs

00:16:48.379 --> 00:16:50.975
takes over the tiny island.

00:16:50.975 --> 00:16:53.268
They take their cues from
a unique combination of

00:16:53.268 --> 00:16:56.031
weather and the movement of the moon.

00:16:56.031 --> 00:16:58.831
As the rains of the
monsoon wet season return

00:16:58.831 --> 00:17:01.160
they begin their journey.

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.232
The crabs arrive at the coast

00:17:03.232 --> 00:17:06.395
where they dig burrows for mating.

00:17:06.395 --> 00:17:09.558
And then the females release
their eggs into the sea

00:17:09.558 --> 00:17:14.130
just as the high tide from
the new moon hits the shore.

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:18.061
These crabs are so reliant
on these seasonal cues

00:17:18.061 --> 00:17:20.721
that if the wet season
doesn't arrive on time

00:17:20.721 --> 00:17:25.271
the crabs will not spawn
until the next lunar month.

00:17:25.271 --> 00:17:27.268
They make their migrations
without much care

00:17:27.268 --> 00:17:30.296
for road rules or
streets, which results in

00:17:30.296 --> 00:17:34.173
incredible and slightly
startling waves of red crabs

00:17:34.173 --> 00:17:36.256
moving across the island.

00:17:39.282 --> 00:17:41.676
In some of the world's coldest climates,

00:17:41.676 --> 00:17:45.420
teams of men and women don
helmets, eye protection,

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:49.400
and warm clothes, and head
out into the snowy terrain

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.097
to prepare for a battle
against the elements

00:17:52.097 --> 00:17:54.170
and each other.

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:57.256
What is this form of frigid
weather fighting, you ask?

00:17:57.256 --> 00:17:59.256
They call it Yukigassen.

00:18:12.618 --> 00:18:15.467
Established in 1988 as a
way to encourage tourism

00:18:15.467 --> 00:18:17.346
to Japan in the winter months,

00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:21.535
what was once a kid's game
is now a hard core sport

00:18:21.535 --> 00:18:23.702
played in eight countries.

00:18:32.668 --> 00:18:34.547
- And we can go and play Yukigassen

00:18:34.547 --> 00:18:36.797
every day or every weekend.

00:18:36.797 --> 00:18:38.472
- [Narrator] Kemijarvi
is famous for holding the

00:18:38.472 --> 00:18:41.889
European Yukigassen tournament each year.

00:18:43.059 --> 00:18:45.474
It is here that three Japanese players

00:18:45.474 --> 00:18:47.891
have flown in for a scrimmage

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:52.727
against three local
Finnish Yukigassen players.

00:18:58.704 --> 00:19:01.367
Yukigassen is a best of three rounds game.

00:19:01.367 --> 00:19:03.457
Players who are hit by a single snowball

00:19:03.457 --> 00:19:05.207
are out of the round.

00:19:06.724 --> 00:19:09.186
To win, players must either steal the flag

00:19:09.186 --> 00:19:11.134
from the opposing team, or eliminate

00:19:11.134 --> 00:19:13.884
all their players with snowballs.

00:19:21.828 --> 00:19:23.888
Teams play with
regulation-approved snowballs

00:19:23.888 --> 00:19:26.082
made by machines.

00:19:26.082 --> 00:19:29.818
The perfect playing condition
is just below freezing,

00:19:29.818 --> 00:19:33.985
so the snowballs have enough
moisture to hold their form.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.813
The sport is not without its hazards.

00:19:55.548 --> 00:19:59.378
The Finnish team might have won this game,

00:19:59.378 --> 00:20:02.616
but win or lose, the
battlefields of Yukigassen

00:20:02.616 --> 00:20:05.616
have the power to unite, not divide.

00:20:25.684 --> 00:20:28.030
With seven billion people on Earth,

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:30.728
it's a wonder that there are
still corners of this planet

00:20:30.728 --> 00:20:32.561
that remain untouched.

00:20:33.596 --> 00:20:36.304
But one man has made it
his life's work to see them

00:20:36.304 --> 00:20:39.657
no matter what danger
the journey may bring.

00:20:39.657 --> 00:20:41.357
Mike Libecki has never been one

00:20:41.357 --> 00:20:43.774
to shy away from a challenge.

00:20:44.864 --> 00:20:48.302
The former National Geographic
Explorer of the Year

00:20:48.302 --> 00:20:52.469
has completed major expeditions
on all seven continents.

00:20:53.728 --> 00:20:55.818
He is a modern day explorer

00:20:55.818 --> 00:20:58.372
who puts his life on the
line to challenge the idea

00:20:58.372 --> 00:21:01.622
of what humans are capable of enduring.

00:21:02.875 --> 00:21:05.514
His goal?
To seek out the Earth's

00:21:05.514 --> 00:21:07.847
last truly untouched places.

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.396
- [Mike] I grew up in California,

00:21:15.396 --> 00:21:18.002
about 30 miles from
Yosemite National Park,

00:21:18.002 --> 00:21:20.102
the center of the universe for climbing.

00:21:20.102 --> 00:21:22.164
My friend's older brother's friend

00:21:22.164 --> 00:21:24.414
happened to take us out
rock climbing one day.

00:21:24.414 --> 00:21:26.380
Totally random, I didn't even know

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:27.794
what rock climbing was.

00:21:27.794 --> 00:21:30.461
And it changed my life that day.

00:21:31.736 --> 00:21:33.578
As soon as I came home from
my first day of climbing

00:21:33.578 --> 00:21:36.542
I was buying a climbing rope,
climbing shoes, harness.

00:21:36.542 --> 00:21:39.194
Climb, climb, climb, that's
all I could think about.

00:21:39.194 --> 00:21:42.623
And that basically evolved
into living in Yosemite,

00:21:42.623 --> 00:21:44.818
climbing El Capitan and
Half Dome and I think

00:21:44.818 --> 00:21:48.985
oh my gosh, I could take this
climbing all over the world.

00:21:50.035 --> 00:21:52.048
- [Narrator] Libecki's
passion for adventure

00:21:52.048 --> 00:21:56.098
has taken him around the
planet to nearly 100 countries.

00:21:56.098 --> 00:21:59.505
But he is not content with
following traditional routes.

00:21:59.505 --> 00:22:03.119
His specialty is bagging first ascents.

00:22:03.119 --> 00:22:06.018
- [Mike] First ascent is
basically climbing a route,

00:22:06.018 --> 00:22:07.710
or going to the top of a mountain,

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:08.944
no one's climbed before.

00:22:08.944 --> 00:22:11.033
So truly going into parts of the planet

00:22:11.033 --> 00:22:13.294
that no human has been to before.

00:22:13.294 --> 00:22:17.377
It really takes every
single thing that you have.

00:22:18.948 --> 00:22:20.980
I mean these aren't just first ascents.

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.518
These are the most remote,
most demanding first ascents

00:22:23.518 --> 00:22:24.904
and climbs on the planet.

00:22:24.904 --> 00:22:26.216
There is no rescue possibility,

00:22:26.216 --> 00:22:28.664
we're 100 percent self-reliant.

00:22:28.664 --> 00:22:31.184
And I'm not seeking danger or death.

00:22:31.184 --> 00:22:33.349
I'm seeking the ultimate challenge

00:22:33.349 --> 00:22:37.388
and the ultimate mystery
of what's out there.

00:22:37.388 --> 00:22:40.001
- [Narrator] From the
blistering heat of Afghanistan.

00:22:40.001 --> 00:22:41.824
- Sure got hot quick.

00:22:41.824 --> 00:22:43.777
- [Narrator] To the
extreme cold of Canada.

00:22:43.777 --> 00:22:47.329
- The weather is getting
worse and it is cold up here.

00:22:47.329 --> 00:22:49.599
- [Narrator] The
climber-turned-explorer's trips

00:22:49.599 --> 00:22:52.469
have taken him through every climate.

00:22:52.469 --> 00:22:55.215
But in his expeditions,
Libecki has learned

00:22:55.215 --> 00:22:57.715
to love conditions like these.

00:22:59.189 --> 00:23:01.084
- Did this incredible
trip to a place called

00:23:01.084 --> 00:23:03.390
Queen Maud Land, Antarctica.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:05.604
The most remote big wall climbing place

00:23:05.604 --> 00:23:06.843
there is on the planet.

00:23:06.843 --> 00:23:08.305
And I think it's technically one of the

00:23:08.305 --> 00:23:10.412
windiest places on the planet.

00:23:10.412 --> 00:23:14.565
Heavy, crazy winds that
are 100, 120 miles an hour.

00:23:14.565 --> 00:23:17.719
That is some very real
deal environment to be in.

00:23:17.719 --> 00:23:18.887
You will freeze quick.

00:23:18.887 --> 00:23:23.054
And so there are these winds
called katabatic winds.

00:23:30.201 --> 00:23:32.833
There is just hammering
and hammering at all times.

00:23:32.833 --> 00:23:34.728
And they come from the South Pole

00:23:34.728 --> 00:23:37.386
and they're pushed out towards the north

00:23:37.386 --> 00:23:40.005
through different channels
around Antarctica.

00:23:40.005 --> 00:23:41.583
And we happened to be in this sort of

00:23:41.583 --> 00:23:43.758
fjord-like valley where
the winds were just

00:23:43.758 --> 00:23:45.491
you know, channeling down and

00:23:45.491 --> 00:23:49.542
it was the most challenging
environment I've ever been in.

00:23:49.542 --> 00:23:52.477
The cool thing about being
in an environment like that

00:23:52.477 --> 00:23:55.233
with the right equipment,
is you start to acclimate

00:23:55.233 --> 00:23:57.523
into these conditions,
you start to get used to

00:23:57.523 --> 00:24:00.311
and you start to understand
how it's working.

00:24:00.311 --> 00:24:03.997
Up here just near the summit
in Queen Maud Land, Antarctica

00:24:03.997 --> 00:24:06.771
on a beautiful, sweet wall.

00:24:06.771 --> 00:24:10.938
You sort of make a friendship
with this environment.

00:24:15.483 --> 00:24:17.763
I have thousands of maps downstairs and

00:24:17.763 --> 00:24:21.039
it's just constant planning
and constant research.

00:24:21.039 --> 00:24:24.307
You know there is an incredible
amount of physical demand.

00:24:24.307 --> 00:24:27.035
I'm carrying a hundred-pound
loads for 80 miles.

00:24:27.035 --> 00:24:29.371
You know, climbing these big walls.

00:24:29.371 --> 00:24:32.362
Enduring all of the weather
and the environments.

00:24:32.362 --> 00:24:34.235
Looks like a storm is upon us.

00:24:34.235 --> 00:24:36.915
We're a little bit
delirious, cold, dehydrated.

00:24:36.915 --> 00:24:38.861
You know one of the things
I have to do is train.

00:24:38.861 --> 00:24:41.807
I have to be physically
fit to have this lifestyle.

00:24:41.807 --> 00:24:46.137
And one of my favorite ways
is to train with my dogs.

00:24:46.137 --> 00:24:49.884
I have a mother and daughter
dog, Scree and Glacier.

00:24:49.884 --> 00:24:52.098
And they're my favorite training partners.

00:24:52.098 --> 00:24:54.046
And we get out in the mountains literally

00:24:54.046 --> 00:24:56.629
every single day that I'm home.

00:24:57.575 --> 00:24:59.154
And you really have to work hard

00:24:59.154 --> 00:25:01.571
or you will suffer out there.

00:25:03.795 --> 00:25:06.082
Being an explorer has a
lot of responsibilities.

00:25:06.082 --> 00:25:08.561
It's not just going out
and climbing and exploring.

00:25:08.561 --> 00:25:11.280
It's getting people excited
to care more about the planet

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:13.188
and being able to share these stories

00:25:13.188 --> 00:25:16.971
about the remote places
or these remote cultures.

00:25:16.971 --> 00:25:19.259
- [Narrator] With 23
more trips in the works,

00:25:19.259 --> 00:25:21.255
Libecki is likely to reach his goal of

00:25:21.255 --> 00:25:23.828
100 expeditions before long.

00:25:23.828 --> 00:25:26.564
- I'm self-diagnosed with OECD, and that's

00:25:26.564 --> 00:25:29.079
obsessive expedition climbing disorder.

00:25:29.079 --> 00:25:30.913
It's my fuel of life.

00:25:30.913 --> 00:25:33.309
And I've often thought
about when will I stop

00:25:33.309 --> 00:25:34.629
going on these expeditions?

00:25:34.629 --> 00:25:37.319
And it's basically when
will the passion stop?

00:25:37.319 --> 00:25:40.425
I hope it never does
because this is a passion.

00:25:40.425 --> 00:25:43.209
This is what I love,
this is what drives me.

00:25:43.209 --> 00:25:44.767
You've gotta live a sweet life.

00:25:44.767 --> 00:25:46.715
Right on, brother!

00:25:46.715 --> 00:25:50.882
First ascent, first time
on the summit, loving life!

00:25:59.491 --> 00:26:01.791
- [Narrator] This is the story of a plant.

00:26:01.791 --> 00:26:04.391
But not just any plant.

00:26:04.391 --> 00:26:07.805
It is the story of a
plant that long, long ago

00:26:07.805 --> 00:26:10.379
once ruled the world.

00:26:10.379 --> 00:26:14.379
A plant that today is the
very last of its kind.

00:26:16.135 --> 00:26:20.107
- It's this plant behind
me, Encephalartos woodii.

00:26:20.107 --> 00:26:22.848
E. Woodii for short, and
I've been looking after it

00:26:22.848 --> 00:26:24.348
for over 20 years.

00:26:25.536 --> 00:26:27.762
It was named for a British botanist,

00:26:27.762 --> 00:26:30.989
John Medley Wood, who
in 1895 discovered it

00:26:30.989 --> 00:26:34.039
growing on a hillside on
the coast of South Africa.

00:26:34.039 --> 00:26:36.727
A strange handsome plant caught his eye

00:26:36.727 --> 00:26:39.170
and he carefully removed
a small portion of it

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.705
and had it shipped all the way to London

00:26:41.705 --> 00:26:44.092
to here, Kew Gardens, where it has been

00:26:44.092 --> 00:26:46.009
for the last 117 years.

00:26:47.092 --> 00:26:50.842
But its history goes
much, much further back.

00:26:52.974 --> 00:26:57.748
You see, Encephalartos Woodii
is what is known as a Cycad.

00:26:57.748 --> 00:27:01.500
And Cycads have been around
for 300 million years.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.768
As the millennium rolled
on, Cycads flourished,

00:27:04.768 --> 00:27:07.210
providing shade for Triceratops,

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:09.366
a perch for Pterodactyls,

00:27:09.366 --> 00:27:12.178
and a tasty snack for Brontosauruses.

00:27:12.178 --> 00:27:14.343
And at one point during the Jurassic,

00:27:14.343 --> 00:27:18.610
Cycads made up 20 percent
of all the plants on Earth

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:21.809
and covered every corner of the globe.

00:27:21.809 --> 00:27:25.647
But the good times couldn't last forever.

00:27:25.647 --> 00:27:27.482
The dinosaurs went extinct.

00:27:27.482 --> 00:27:30.200
Ice ages came and went.

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:33.285
New modern plants like
conifers and fruit trees

00:27:33.285 --> 00:27:35.385
started pushing Cycads out.

00:27:35.385 --> 00:27:39.320
And the once-proud population
of E. Woodiis were reduced,

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:41.303
and reduced, and reduced,

00:27:41.303 --> 00:27:44.619
until there was possibly only one left.

00:27:44.619 --> 00:27:47.528
One single solitary E. Woodii

00:27:47.528 --> 00:27:49.466
growing quietly on a hillside.

00:27:49.466 --> 00:27:52.665
Which brings us right
back to John Medley Wood.

00:27:52.665 --> 00:27:55.059
At the time he had no way of knowing

00:27:55.059 --> 00:27:57.842
just how rare his discovery was.

00:27:57.842 --> 00:28:00.626
But expedition after expedition,

00:28:00.626 --> 00:28:04.884
in search of more E. Woodii,
have proved fruitless.

00:28:04.884 --> 00:28:07.419
You see, Cycads are dioecious,

00:28:07.419 --> 00:28:10.432
meaning you need separate
male and female plants

00:28:10.432 --> 00:28:12.190
to create a new one.

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:16.301
And this one happens to be a
male, a true lonely bachelor.

00:28:16.301 --> 00:28:18.899
If a female mate cannot be found,

00:28:18.899 --> 00:28:22.661
it really will be the last of its kind.

00:28:22.661 --> 00:28:25.397
To this day researchers are still looking.

00:28:25.397 --> 00:28:29.564
After all, it's a big world,
and might just be a chance.

00:28:32.027 --> 00:28:36.194
In the meantime, he'll have
me to keep him company.

00:28:45.157 --> 00:28:47.029
- [Narrator] The oceans
occupy over two-thirds

00:28:47.029 --> 00:28:50.733
of our planet, but they're in trouble.

00:28:50.733 --> 00:28:53.621
Coral reefs are dying at an alarming rate

00:28:53.621 --> 00:28:55.293
and the fish species that rely on these

00:28:55.293 --> 00:28:58.210
aquatic environments are suffering.

00:28:59.863 --> 00:29:02.530
So what if the solution was art?

00:29:04.451 --> 00:29:05.657
- I think what's really interesting about

00:29:05.657 --> 00:29:07.263
having a museum under water,

00:29:07.263 --> 00:29:09.981
no, it's not just about its artistic value,

00:29:09.981 --> 00:29:12.192
it's also about biology

00:29:12.192 --> 00:29:15.963
and it's science, the
living breathing entities.

00:29:15.963 --> 00:29:17.978
- [Narrator] By putting
his sculptures under water,

00:29:17.978 --> 00:29:20.355
artist Jason DeCaries Taylor has become

00:29:20.355 --> 00:29:22.662
the ocean's unlikely hero.

00:29:22.662 --> 00:29:26.566
His installations are providing
new homes for marine life

00:29:26.566 --> 00:29:30.733
and his art is giving the ocean
a fighting chance to thrive.

00:29:34.225 --> 00:29:36.799
- I consider myself first
and foremost a sculptor,

00:29:36.799 --> 00:29:40.219
an artist, that's my
background and training.

00:29:40.219 --> 00:29:42.471
And I've done a lot of
snorkeling and diving.

00:29:42.471 --> 00:29:44.645
When I was young we moved
around the world as a family.

00:29:44.645 --> 00:29:47.775
We lived in Malaysia and
Thailand and we used to

00:29:47.775 --> 00:29:50.713
explore all the coral reefs along there.

00:29:50.713 --> 00:29:52.985
For me, being under
water has always been like a

00:29:52.985 --> 00:29:55.721
kind of religious experience.

00:29:55.721 --> 00:29:58.637
I was very much fascinated
in working with the sea,

00:29:58.637 --> 00:30:01.773
using my work for not just
for artistic purposes,

00:30:01.773 --> 00:30:05.003
but also as a tool for conservation.

00:30:05.003 --> 00:30:06.665
- [Narrator] His big idea?

00:30:06.665 --> 00:30:08.955
Place his eccentric sculptures under water

00:30:08.955 --> 00:30:12.821
so that coral and other sea
creatures can make them home.

00:30:12.821 --> 00:30:14.361
- I started with small projects

00:30:14.361 --> 00:30:16.859
and I realized that,
you know, as soon as you

00:30:16.859 --> 00:30:19.291
immerse your works into
this new environment

00:30:19.291 --> 00:30:20.849
everything completely changes.

00:30:20.849 --> 00:30:24.251
They are transformed;
they really take on the

00:30:24.251 --> 00:30:27.168
characteristics of the environment.

00:30:35.223 --> 00:30:37.845
I call it a museum for sort
of many different reasons.

00:30:37.845 --> 00:30:41.016
I think when we put something in a museum

00:30:41.016 --> 00:30:43.269
we have a different relationship with it,

00:30:43.269 --> 00:30:46.062
we want to protect it and
we want to cherish it.

00:30:46.062 --> 00:30:48.805
We want to conserve it.

00:30:48.805 --> 00:30:50.585
- [Narrator] Over the last 10 years,

00:30:50.585 --> 00:30:52.758
Jason has opened three other museums

00:30:52.758 --> 00:30:54.279
throughout the Caribbean.

00:30:54.279 --> 00:30:58.446
In 2016 he turned his attention
to the Atlantic Ocean.

00:30:59.705 --> 00:31:01.195
- We are currently here in my studio,

00:31:01.195 --> 00:31:05.689
which is in Lanzarote,
part of the Canary Islands.

00:31:05.689 --> 00:31:07.665
I'm always looking for
the sort of new challenge,

00:31:07.665 --> 00:31:11.057
the new adventure, a new
environment for my work.

00:31:11.057 --> 00:31:12.747
There's no corals here.

00:31:12.747 --> 00:31:14.677
When I first came here, you know,

00:31:14.677 --> 00:31:16.425
I dived the sites we were looking at

00:31:16.425 --> 00:31:18.353
and it really was really
desolate, you know?

00:31:18.353 --> 00:31:22.647
Just swimming over sand
for quite some time.

00:31:22.647 --> 00:31:24.937
- [Narrator] Jason will
create 300 sculptures

00:31:24.937 --> 00:31:29.563
as part of Museo Atlantico,
his new underwater museum.

00:31:29.563 --> 00:31:31.149
And while his art has the ability

00:31:31.149 --> 00:31:36.059
to regenerate marine life, his
process is far from simple.

00:31:36.059 --> 00:31:39.185
- Finding the site is
the most important thing.

00:31:39.185 --> 00:31:40.781
We're always looking for areas where

00:31:40.781 --> 00:31:44.021
we're not inflicting any damage
on the existing marine life.

00:31:44.021 --> 00:31:48.249
We try to work with areas
that are completely desolate.

00:31:48.249 --> 00:31:50.395
My sculptures are based on life models.

00:31:50.395 --> 00:31:52.181
And so people come to the studio,

00:31:52.181 --> 00:31:54.861
we actually cover them
in a dental solution,

00:31:54.861 --> 00:31:57.169
make an impression of their bodies

00:31:57.169 --> 00:31:59.307
and then with that we
actually fill the molds

00:31:59.307 --> 00:32:01.595
with a special type of marine cement.

00:32:01.595 --> 00:32:04.188
And this cement is pH neutral,

00:32:04.188 --> 00:32:06.745
it doesn't damage the environment any way,

00:32:06.745 --> 00:32:08.607
and it actually provides
the perfect habitat

00:32:08.607 --> 00:32:12.539
for marine life to attach and to grow.

00:32:12.539 --> 00:32:15.510
I'll make certain ledges, substrates

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.363
that will actually attract sponges,

00:32:17.363 --> 00:32:20.033
little holes and nooks
and crannies that will

00:32:20.033 --> 00:32:23.759
attract different species
of fish and crustacean.

00:32:23.759 --> 00:32:27.207
We then have to transport
them to the site.

00:32:27.207 --> 00:32:29.790
They're pieces that we
worked hard at in the studio

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:32.950
for many months, they have
become very personal to you.

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:35.116
And then it's kind of this process of

00:32:35.116 --> 00:32:36.477
letting go, saying goodbye,

00:32:36.477 --> 00:32:39.560
they're about to start a new journey.

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:47.221
So the sculptures change rapidly
once they're under water.

00:32:47.221 --> 00:32:51.107
Here in Lanzaroti we've seen
some really incredible results.

00:32:51.107 --> 00:32:55.674
Just after two weeks there were
significant transformations.

00:32:55.674 --> 00:32:57.754
We've seen a lot of
different types of algae,

00:32:57.754 --> 00:32:59.692
we've seen some sponges adhere to them.

00:32:59.692 --> 00:33:02.997
Marine life is actually
changing the surfaces,

00:33:02.997 --> 00:33:06.189
adding patinas, morphing the forms.

00:33:06.189 --> 00:33:10.739
And they make the whole, sort
of, sculpture come alive.

00:33:10.739 --> 00:33:13.041
But I wasn't quite expecting how

00:33:13.041 --> 00:33:14.675
dramatic they would change and how

00:33:14.675 --> 00:33:17.418
beautiful a colonization
would actually look.

00:33:17.418 --> 00:33:20.876
You can't get a better
co-collaborator than nature.

00:33:20.876 --> 00:33:23.623
- [Narrator] Jason's art
doesn't just support marine life

00:33:23.623 --> 00:33:25.419
and promote ocean health.

00:33:25.419 --> 00:33:29.209
It attracts tourists, which
can be a very powerful tool

00:33:29.209 --> 00:33:30.909
for raising awareness.

00:33:30.909 --> 00:33:33.075
- [Jason] I kind of
really like the idea of

00:33:33.075 --> 00:33:36.761
instead of us always being
apart from natural environments,

00:33:36.761 --> 00:33:39.299
it's kind of we are nature, we are all

00:33:39.299 --> 00:33:41.359
part of the same system.

00:33:41.359 --> 00:33:45.083
By putting my work into these
difficult-to-access places,

00:33:45.083 --> 00:33:48.085
I hope people will have
a better understanding

00:33:48.085 --> 00:33:50.613
and really realize what
an incredible resource

00:33:50.613 --> 00:33:53.910
our oceans are and how
desperately urgent it is

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:55.993
to try and protect them.

00:34:03.019 --> 00:34:04.999
- [Narrator] Wayne
Adams and Catherine King

00:34:04.999 --> 00:34:06.859
don't live in your typical house,

00:34:06.859 --> 00:34:10.233
but this is their dream
home, Freedom Cove.

00:34:10.233 --> 00:34:13.816
A floating island,
completely off the grid.

00:34:14.839 --> 00:34:17.149
It's nearly an hour to the nearest town,

00:34:17.149 --> 00:34:19.485
Tofino, British Columbia, an area known

00:34:19.485 --> 00:34:23.330
for its mild summers and wicked winters.

00:34:23.330 --> 00:34:24.913
But this married couple doesn't mind

00:34:24.913 --> 00:34:28.163
the elemental extreme, they embrace it.

00:34:36.454 --> 00:34:38.793
- [Wayne] We live in a secluded cove.

00:34:38.793 --> 00:34:41.252
The only options we have
to get here is by water,

00:34:41.252 --> 00:34:43.796
there are no road accesses.

00:34:43.796 --> 00:34:45.879
The water is our highway.

00:34:47.684 --> 00:34:51.436
- 24 years ago we both
knew that we wanted to live

00:34:51.436 --> 00:34:52.603
out in nature.

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:57.250
We have carved a piece of
the world out for ourselves.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.000
We can live uniquely, differently

00:35:01.136 --> 00:35:03.958
than anyone else on the planet.

00:35:03.958 --> 00:35:06.294
If we had to survive on just

00:35:06.294 --> 00:35:09.294
exactly what we have here, we could.

00:35:10.292 --> 00:35:15.054
We have our main living
house, we have our wood shed.

00:35:15.054 --> 00:35:19.452
We have four greenhouses, I am
able to grow all year round.

00:35:19.452 --> 00:35:22.540
This is a temperate rainforest climate,

00:35:22.540 --> 00:35:25.570
so we do get snow, it will freeze, but

00:35:25.570 --> 00:35:29.737
it never lasts for long so all
the plants can tolerate that.

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:35.427
Everything that you see here
in our home is floating.

00:35:36.494 --> 00:35:40.728
We are tied to shore with
lines, we are not anchored.

00:35:40.728 --> 00:35:43.140
The way Wayne has the ropes arranged

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:46.856
is what slows down the impact of the wind.

00:35:46.856 --> 00:35:51.292
We get hurricane-force
winds in here in big storms.

00:35:51.292 --> 00:35:54.236
It's a narrow entrance into this cove.

00:35:54.236 --> 00:35:58.284
The wind rockets in and whips
around and changes direction.

00:35:58.284 --> 00:36:00.534
So it can be pretty fierce.

00:36:06.728 --> 00:36:09.249
- Everything's done with
a handsaw and hammer.

00:36:09.249 --> 00:36:10.254
No power tools.

00:36:10.254 --> 00:36:12.364
I know every board and nail by name.

00:36:12.364 --> 00:36:16.289
It's about 500 tons that I'm floating.

00:36:16.289 --> 00:36:20.456
I don't get sea sick, when I
go to town I get land sick.

00:36:21.484 --> 00:36:23.776
The thing about living in Clayoquot Sound

00:36:23.776 --> 00:36:26.184
on the west coast of Vancouver Island is

00:36:26.184 --> 00:36:28.064
it's the richest biomass on Earth.

00:36:28.064 --> 00:36:31.362
So the opportunity to
fish for dinner is big.

00:36:31.362 --> 00:36:33.186
And I just can get in my
canoe and I can paddle out,

00:36:33.186 --> 00:36:36.282
in ten minutes I can catch a fish.

00:36:36.282 --> 00:36:38.202
But when it's windy
and too rough out there

00:36:38.202 --> 00:36:42.369
I can just lay on the couch
and fish out of the house.

00:36:46.428 --> 00:36:49.032
- [Catherine] There's no
quick fix living out here.

00:36:49.032 --> 00:36:51.434
You have to do the work
to get what you want

00:36:51.434 --> 00:36:52.684
if you want it.

00:36:53.534 --> 00:36:56.174
- [Wayne] It was a great
opportunity to actually move

00:36:56.174 --> 00:36:59.120
away from the city, to see if we could

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:00.812
prosper out here.

00:37:00.812 --> 00:37:04.229
Now 24 years later, we're still doing it.

00:37:05.106 --> 00:37:08.224
- I can't imagine living any other way.

00:37:08.224 --> 00:37:10.557
I feel completely fulfilled.

00:37:11.464 --> 00:37:12.528
- Well that's good, honey.

00:37:12.528 --> 00:37:13.810
(Catherine laughs)

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:16.977
That's really nice, I'm working on it.

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:32.398
- [Narrator] Roman Lacombe
is a man on a mission.

00:37:32.398 --> 00:37:34.310
- Air pollution is an
environmental issue, but

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:36.293
most of all, it's a health crisis.

00:37:36.293 --> 00:37:39.434
- [Narrator] More than five
million people die every year

00:37:39.434 --> 00:37:42.257
from pollution-related illnesses.

00:37:42.257 --> 00:37:44.575
Roman wants to change that.

00:37:44.575 --> 00:37:46.675
He has designed a system to help humans

00:37:46.675 --> 00:37:49.228
learn more about the air they breathe.

00:37:49.228 --> 00:37:53.646
And he did it in the most
unconventional way imaginable.

00:37:53.646 --> 00:37:56.656
Using pigeons wearing tiny backpacks.

00:37:56.656 --> 00:38:00.296
- I will never look at
pigeons, ever again,

00:38:00.296 --> 00:38:01.796
like I did before.

00:38:08.568 --> 00:38:12.016
- [Narrator] London, like
many cities, has a problem.

00:38:12.016 --> 00:38:12.849
Pollution.

00:38:14.009 --> 00:38:18.176
It's so bad here it kills
nearly 10,000 citizens a year.

00:38:20.963 --> 00:38:22.911
- The problem about the
weather and pollution

00:38:22.911 --> 00:38:25.530
is they interact together
and sometimes create

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:27.417
episodes of very high pollution.

00:38:27.417 --> 00:38:29.670
- [Narrator] London weather
is notoriously fickle

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:32.340
and pollution levels
change with the weather.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:36.507
Rains wash up pollutants,
and winds sweep them away.

00:38:37.678 --> 00:38:41.736
Roman wanted to make
the invisible, visible.

00:38:41.736 --> 00:38:44.366
To do that he found a
way for people to know

00:38:44.366 --> 00:38:47.482
when the air is particularly toxic.

00:38:47.482 --> 00:38:49.815
Enter the Pigeon Air Patrol.

00:38:52.138 --> 00:38:55.108
- [Rome] The Pigeon Air Patrol
was this brilliant crazy idea

00:38:55.108 --> 00:38:58.280
to fly a flock of pigeons
over the city of London

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:01.584
with tiny little backpacks
to measure air pollution.

00:39:01.584 --> 00:39:04.160
- [Narrator] Roman outfitted
a flock of 10 birds

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:08.327
to fly around London to monitor
air quality in real time.

00:39:13.584 --> 00:39:16.510
They wore special backpacks
equipped with sensors

00:39:16.510 --> 00:39:20.749
to measure pollutants like
ozone and nitrogen dioxide.

00:39:20.749 --> 00:39:23.105
And they weren't just ordinary birds.

00:39:23.105 --> 00:39:25.661
They were highly-trained racing pigeons.

00:39:25.661 --> 00:39:27.189
- [Roman] Well we worked with Brian,

00:39:27.189 --> 00:39:29.213
expert pigeon fancier who's been

00:39:29.213 --> 00:39:31.141
very widely recognized in that field.

00:39:31.141 --> 00:39:33.477
These are professional pigeons, they race,

00:39:33.477 --> 00:39:36.547
they know how to always home
back to their home base.

00:39:36.547 --> 00:39:40.714
We have an entire patrol of
pigeons, Coco, Norbert, Julius.

00:39:52.562 --> 00:39:54.911
- [Narrator] When the
pigeons took to the sky,

00:39:54.911 --> 00:39:57.513
the data they collected
was instantly accessible

00:39:57.513 --> 00:39:59.091
to anyone on the ground.

00:39:59.091 --> 00:40:01.893
Citizens could tweet the Pigeon Air Patrol

00:40:01.893 --> 00:40:04.201
and the nearest backpack
sensor would respond

00:40:04.201 --> 00:40:07.034
with readings from low to extreme.

00:40:08.315 --> 00:40:09.987
- Thanks to this, people tuned in

00:40:09.987 --> 00:40:12.124
to know when's a good
time to go running and

00:40:12.124 --> 00:40:14.547
when you can bike without
worrying about pollution,

00:40:14.547 --> 00:40:16.978
or when you should really take extra care

00:40:16.978 --> 00:40:18.707
about the air you breathe.

00:40:18.707 --> 00:40:20.713
The Pigeon Air Patrol was
just a starting point.

00:40:20.713 --> 00:40:22.271
So of course the Pigeon Air Patrol was not

00:40:22.271 --> 00:40:23.999
fixing pollution in London.

00:40:23.999 --> 00:40:27.647
But by bringing attention from
everyone about this topic,

00:40:27.647 --> 00:40:30.411
we make it easier for
citizens to demand change.

00:40:30.411 --> 00:40:32.120
And so we make it easier
for everyone involved

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:33.309
to say there's an issue there,

00:40:33.309 --> 00:40:35.219
we should recognize it,
we should address it,

00:40:35.219 --> 00:40:37.136
and then we can fix it.

00:40:48.615 --> 00:40:50.838
If we could only make more people care

00:40:50.838 --> 00:40:53.796
about what they breathe, then
of course it becomes easier

00:40:53.796 --> 00:40:57.058
to collectively address the issue.

00:40:57.058 --> 00:40:59.514
And that's why the pigeons
are the heroes of the story

00:40:59.514 --> 00:41:01.582
and are so important.

00:41:01.582 --> 00:41:03.105
They really carry this message,

00:41:03.105 --> 00:41:04.982
that if we can hear more
about our environments

00:41:04.982 --> 00:41:08.982
then we'll make our health
better for everyone.

